id: pm-wzmbvn
title: "Published-package verification gives up before npm metadata propagates: two consecutive scheduled cuts were recorded as failed and re-run by hand while the artifact was already live"
description: "## Observed\nOn 2026-09-11 (Release run 34576128355) and 2026-09-12 (Release run 34681372798) the tag-push Release workflow published the package successfully, then failed its own \"Verify published package on npm, npx, and bunx\" step on the first attempt. scripts/release/verify-published-release.mjs polls npm view for the exact version with a default of 20 attempts 15 seconds apart (about 5 minutes) and gave up with npm error code E404 \"No match found for version\". The npm registry's own time stamp for the version shows the metadata became visible 8 min 14 s (2026.9.11) and 6 min 10 s (2026.9.12) after the publish step finished. Both days the maintainer re-ran the failed job by hand about ten minutes later; attempt 2 passed on its first poll and the blocker issue (#1235, #1239) was closed.\n\n## Why it matters\nThe scheduled auto-release run watches the Release workflow and records release-workflow as its failing stage, so two correct, on-time publications count as scheduled failures in the rolling rate pm-e70zh5 measures, a blocker issue is opened for each, and a human must intervene to finish a release that already succeeded. The verifier's ceiling is a constant fitted to an earlier registry latency; nothing compares it with the lag the registry actually exhibits.\n\n## Non-goals\nNot a retry of npm publish (publication was correct both days). Not the direct-main analyzer refusal (pm-2ksptv). Not the same-day second-release policy (pm-uenmu9)."
